# Orrery — Screens, menus, and audio

This file defines the screens the game moves between, the two panels drawn
over a run, the menus, and the audio cues. The editor screen's own regions
and behavior are in `specs/editor.md`, the two modes and their select screens
in `specs/modes/campaign.md` and `specs/modes/extras.md`, and the input in
`specs/controls.md`. Every figure and every piece of screen copy below
carries the name this specification gives it.

## Presentation

Orrery fixes no palette, no font, and no background. The sprites, effects,
and sounds it ships are produced during this build, as `specs/assets.md`
states. What else is fixed about appearance is stated as legibility, in
`specs/field.md`, `specs/parts.md`, and `specs/editor.md`. One requirement is
this file's own: every piece of text a screen shows is legible against
whatever sits behind it at the logical stage size `STAGE_W x STAGE_H`
(`1280 x 720`).

## Screens

`state.screen` names the screen the game is in: `title`, `howto`, `select`,
or `editor`. `state.mode` is `campaign` or `extras`, and decides which course
the `select` and `editor` screens serve.

### `title`

The main menu, and where the game opens.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `TITLE_TEXT` | `ORRERY` |
| Tagline | `TAGLINE_TEXT` | `SET THE HEAVENS TURNING` |
| Menu | `TITLE_ITEMS` | `CAMPAIGN`, `EXTRAS`, `HOW TO PLAY`, in that order |

The menu's items are stacked one above the next under the title and tagline.
The item at `state.menuIndex` is highlighted and drawn distinctly from the
others. `menuIndex` is `0` on arriving at the title. `up` and `down` move the
highlight by one item and wrap at both ends. `back` does nothing.

`confirm` takes the highlighted item:

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `CAMPAIGN` | Sets `state.mode` to `campaign` and goes to `select`. |
| `EXTRAS` | Sets `state.mode` to `extras` and goes to `select`. |
| `HOW TO PLAY` | Goes to `howto`, page `0`. |

Reaching `title` discards nothing: progress, records, and the per-challenge
machines of `specs/editor.md` are unchanged by the visit.

### `howto`

How to play, told in a player's words rather than as rules of a system, over
`HOWTO_PAGES` (`5`) pages. `state.howtoPage` names the page shown, `0` on
arriving; `left` and `right` move it by one, stopping at `0` and
`HOWTO_PAGES - 1`, and the current page is indicated on screen. `confirm` and
`back` both return to `title` with `menuIndex` `0`. The pages, in order:

1. The sky: the hex field, motes and their classes, filaments,
   constellations, and that a challenge asks for products made from reagents.
2. The machine: rises and sets, arms and grippers, the wheel, track, and each
   sigil's engraving in one line apiece.
3. The tape: instructions, how every tape loops on one shared period, and
   how to write a tape, naming the keys.
4. Running: play, step, and speed, naming the keys; that motes collide; and
   that a fault stops the machine and where to read what went wrong.
5. Finishing: the target tally, the three metrics, and that lower is better.

Where a page names a key it names the key itself rather than the action it
fires, spelled as the keyboard spells it: a letter key by its capital letter
standing on its own, `Space`, `Delete`, and `Backspace` by their names, and
the comma and period keys by the character each types or by that character's
name.

### `select`

The list of the current mode's challenges. `specs/modes/campaign.md` and
`specs/modes/extras.md` define it for their modes; the two share one screen
and differ only in the list they show and the locking they apply.

### `editor`

The machine screen `specs/editor.md` defines, hosting both editing and the
run. The two panels below are drawn over it while a run is in the matching
status, and the field stays visible behind them. Neither panel hides what it
covers: at every hex under a panel the machine, the motes, and any effect
playing there show through it, so what changes beneath a panel changes the
frame.

## The solved panel

While `sim.status` is `complete`, a panel is drawn over the run showing
`SOLVED_TITLE_TEXT` (`CHALLENGE COMPLETE`), the finished run's three metrics,
the challenge's records with any new best marked, and a vertical menu built
from `SOLVED_ITEMS` (`NEXT CHALLENGE`, `KEEP TINKERING`, `BACK TO SELECT`, in
that order). `NEXT CHALLENGE` is offered only when the mode has a challenge
after this one; the menu is otherwise the remaining two items in the same
order. `state.menuIndex` is `0` on arriving, `up` and `down` move it with
wrapping, and `confirm` takes the item:

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `NEXT CHALLENGE` | Opens the next challenge of the mode in the editor. |
| `KEEP TINKERING` | Stops the run and returns to editing, machine intact. |
| `BACK TO SELECT` | Goes to `select`. |

`back` stops the run and returns to editing, machine intact, exactly as
`KEEP TINKERING` does. The finished machine stays visible behind the panel.

A challenge loaded directly through the surface of `specs/instrumentation.md`
belongs to no course, so its panel never offers `NEXT CHALLENGE`, and
`BACK TO SELECT` goes to the current `state.mode`'s select screen with every
progress figure and record untouched.

## The fault display

While `sim.status` is `faulted`, the field shows the machine frozen at the
cycle the fault stopped, the parts and motes `specs/simulation.md` names in
the fault drawn visibly distinct from the rest, and a banner naming which of
the `FAULTS` of `specs/simulation.md` stopped the run. The banner's wording
is the build's own. `back` returns to editing, as `specs/simulation.md`
states.

## What advances on each screen

`state.simTime` accumulates the frame's delta time on every update, whatever
the screen, and input is read on every screen. Beyond that:

| Screen | What advances |
| --- | --- |
| `title`, `howto`, `select` | Nothing. |
| `editor` | The run, while `sim.status` is `running`, as `specs/simulation.md` defines. |

## Menu navigation

Menus and select lists are worked from the keyboard alone, through the
registered actions of `specs/controls.md`: `up` and `down` move the
highlight, `confirm` takes the highlighted item, and `back` leaves the
screen. `left` and `right` turn the `howto` pages. `mute` is read on every
screen.

## Audio

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The audio layer is part of the runtime you write, and what it plays are the
produced files `specs/assets.md` lists. Define and play exactly the seven
cues in `CUES`, under exactly these names:
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus, each cue bound with `api.audio.load`
to the produced file `specs/assets.md` lists. Define and play exactly the
seven cues in `CUES`, under exactly these names:
{{else}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus, each cue bound in the instance's
`initialize` with `api.audio.load` to the produced file `specs/assets.md`
lists, and played from a tick through `world.audio`. Define and play exactly
the seven cues in `CUES`, under exactly these names:
{{/if}}

| Cue | Constant | Played when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `place` | `CUES.place` | A part is placed or moved. |
| `erase` | `CUES.erase` | A part is removed. |
| `start` | `CUES.start` | A run starts. |
| `halt` | `CUES.halt` | A run faults. |
| `constellation` | `CUES.constellation` | A set consumes one or more constellations. |
| `complete` | `CUES.complete` | The run completes. |
| `music` | `CUES.music` | Loops from the first frame, on every screen. |

The first six are one-shot cues.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Each is a distinct short sound, played on the frame its event happens, from
`update`, and at most once on that frame, however many of the event fired
within it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Each is a distinct short sound, played on the frame its event happens, from
`update`, and at most once on that frame, however many of the event fired
within it.
{{else}}
Each is a distinct short sound, played on the frame its event happens, from
the game mode's tick, and at most once on that frame, however many of the
event fired within it.
{{/if}}

Every cue is played from a frame, so an event raised outside one — an edit
a pointer pose of `specs/instrumentation.md` commits between frames —
sounds on the next frame advanced rather than at the call. No other
operation of that surface raises an event at all, as that file states.

`LOOPING_CUES` holds the one cue that loops until stopped rather than playing
once. `music` is looping on every frame the game runs, on `title`, `howto`,
`select`, and `editor` alike, in every sim status. The cues are the same in
both modes.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Muting and the first-interaction unlock belong to the runtime. The game binds
the `mute` action to the runtime's mute bit and toggles it from any screen,
then mirrors that bit into `state.muted` every frame. The game stays fully
playable with sound muted.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Muting and the first-gesture unlock belong to the engine. The game binds the
`mute` action to `api.audio.setMuted` and toggles it from any screen, then
mirrors `api.audio.muted()` into `state.muted` every frame. The game stays
fully playable with sound muted.
{{else}}
Muting and the first-gesture unlock belong to the engine. The game binds the
`mute` action to `world.audio.setMuted` and toggles it from any screen, then
mirrors `world.audio.muted()` into `state.muted` every frame. The game stays
fully playable with sound muted.
{{/if}}

Sound is on when the game starts: the mute bit is off on the first frame, so
`state.muted` reports `false` until the `mute` action is first pressed, and
that first press mutes.

## Out of scope

- Network or online play.
- Persistence of progress, records, machines, or settings between sessions.
  Each session starts fresh.
